window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2026-06-3", "source": "2026年06月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose you are invited to put forward a proposal to the university on how to make students' campus life easier. You are now to write an essay to make your proposal. You will have 30 minutes to write the essay. You should write at least 120 words but not more than 180 words.", "sample": "I am honored to submit a proposal on how to make students' campus life easier. As daily routines grow more\ndemanding, small improvements in campus services can save students considerable time and energy.\nFirst, the university could develop a one-stop mobile app that integrates course schedules, room bookings, and\nexam arrangements. This would spare students the trouble of switching between multiple platforms. Second,\nextending library opening hours and adding more self-study spaces during examination periods would reduce\novercrowding. Third, improving shuttle-bus frequency between teaching buildings and dormitories would\nmake commuting less stressful.\nThese practical measures, if implemented, would significantly ease students' daily pressures. I sincerely hope\nthe university will consider this proposal and take steps toward a more convenient campus life."}, "translation": {"prompt": "在中国传统文化中，亲朋好友之间互赠礼物是人际交往的一种重要礼节 (etiquette) 。赠送礼物不仅是物质的馈赠，更是情感的表达，是维系人际关系的重要纽带。一份精心挑选、符合接受者喜好的礼物，往往能加深彼此的感情。中国人遵循\"礼尚往来\" (mutually exchanging courtesies)的传统，接受礼物时会表达谢意，并适时回赠礼物，以增进彼此的融洽关系。", "sample": "In traditional Chinese culture, exchanging gifts among relatives and close friends is an important form of etiquette in interpersonal communication. Giving gifts is not only a material offering, but also an expression of affection, and an important bond that maintains interpersonal relationships. Such a carefully chosen gift that suits the recipient's preferences can often deepen mutual affection. Chinese people follow the tradition of reciprocity (mutually exchanging courtesies): when receiving gifts, they express their gratitude and return gifts at an appropriate time, so as to enhance their harmonious relationship."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "Junk-food lovers who try to cut back on fries or chocolate may experience symptoms similar to drug withdrawal, a new study suggests.\nResearchers found that people attempting to cut down on eating highly processed foods experience some of the same  __26__  and psychological symptoms—such as mood swings, anxiety, headaches and poor sleep—as those  __27__  smoking cigarettes or using drugs, according to the study.\nThe new study  __28__  the first evidence that these withdrawal-like symptoms can occur when people cut down on highly processed foods, said lead study author Erica Schulte.\nBased on the participants' self-reported symptoms, withdrawal symptoms were  __29__  more intense between the second and fifth days after attempting to reduce junk-food consumption, which  __30__  the time span people live through during drug withdrawal.\nThe idea that food may be addictive after \"heavy\" use by some individuals is a  __31__  subject, Schulte said. Although prior studies in animals and humans have shown some biological and behavioral similarities between substance-use disorders and addictive-like consumption of highly processed foods, no studies have looked at whether reducing junk food can  __32__  withdrawal symptoms in people, she noted. Raising  __33__  that people may experience irritability(烦躁) or headaches when cutting down on junk food can help individuals  __34__  coping strategies in advance, Schulte noted. The findings may also  __35__  light on the barriers people face when changing eating habits—barriers that may play a role in people dropping out of treatments, she said.", "matchIntro": "Giving a better phone interview", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "These days, phone interviews are an unavoidable part of the job interview process, and for good reason: They save time and effort. But that doesn't mean that phone calls require zero energy on the part of the candidate. Many companies treat phone screening as the official first round of the hiring process. That means candidates are expected to go into them prepared with as much information as possible about the company, position, and their own skills and strengths."}, {"label": "B", "text": "A big mistake people often make is talking about their personal lives. Don't talk about your personal life unless you're directly asked a question about what you like to do in your off-hours. \"The point of a phone interview is to focus on getting to know a candidate's professional experience and goals,\" says Mckenzie Roark, campus talent specialist at Lithko Contracting. \"A recruiter is trying to see if they are the best fit for a role, and learning about their personal life doesn't help. For example, when asked where you see yourself in five years, we don't want to know that you hope to be married or that you want to buy a new house. That is nice, but that isn't relative to anything professional.\""}, {"label": "C", "text": "It might be tempting to cross something off your to-do list while on a phone interview, but recruiters and hiring managers can easily tell if your attention is elsewhere. Many interviewers are annoyed to see people who decide to multitask while on the phone interview. There are candidates who wash dishes, go for walks, or do grocery shopping during the interview. Needless to say, this doesn't reflect well on your level of interest in the position you're interviewing for."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The question regarding pay is also best avoided. To be frank, it's simply too early in the process for you to be the one who brings up salary expectations. \"Chances are if a candidate is participating in a phone interview, this is the first time they have talked with the company, and the first call isn't the appropriate time to talk about 'what's in it for you,'\" says Justina Strnad, the Talent Acquisition Manager for Shiftgig. \"Trust me, if you are a great candidate and make it to the next step, the hiring team is going to be very transparent about what's in it for you later on.\""}, {"label": "E", "text": "\"After wrapping up a phone interview, it is typical that the interviewer will ask the candidate if they have any questions. I can't stress this enough: ALWAYS ask questions,\" says Roark. \"If we have had a great phone interview and then we wrap up and they don't have any questions for me, it pretty much ruins the whole interview. It tells me that the candidate is uninterested in the role, which in reality might not be the case at all,\" she notes. But surely, if you're interested in a job, you can think of something to ask your interviewer."}, {"label": "F", "text": "It seems basic, but surprisingly, a lot of people are late to phone interviews. \"About a quarter of the people with whom I schedule phone interviews aren't on time,\" says Sophie Cikovsky, who handles U.S. recruiting for Infinite Global. \"While this bothers me personally, it's also indicative of someone who isn't very detail-oriented,\" she explains. \"In order to identify this early in the hiring process, I started asking all candidates a few years ago to call me as opposed to calling them at an agreed-upon time. That way, if I hear from them at 1:13 p.m. or 12:49 p.m. instead of our planned 1:00 p.m. interview time, I have an early indicator that they might not be a great fit.\""}, {"label": "G", "text": "\"Make sure you test your headset and connection before dialing in,\" recommends Roark. \"There is nothing more frustrating for a recruiter who has a structured interview guide in place having to repeatedly ask the same question over and over because they could not understand your answer due to dropped signals.\" Test-call a friend beforehand, or even call yourself from a landline if necessary; it will take less than a minute."}, {"label": "H", "text": "You might be eager to get your point across or talk about your experience, but interrupting the interviewer is awkward and rude. \"Interviewing can be stressful, and sometimes that stress manifests itself in speaking too fast, speaking too loud, talking over the interviewer, or attempting to answer the interviewer's question before they have actually finished asking the question,\" says Chris Dardis, a recruiting expert. \"Don't do this.\" There's a big difference between being assertive(自信的) and being aggressive, and interviewers can always recognize it."}, {"label": "I", "text": "It's tough not to say things like \"um,\" \"uh,\" and \"like\" in everyday speech, but these verbal habits become much more pronounced when speaking on the phone, says Dardis. \"In face-to-face interviews, they're not as noticeable, because there are other things like your hair, suit, or body language to distract people.\" But in a phone interview, the only thing you have to go on is what you say and how you say it. \"That's why it's so important to eliminate these words from your speech when doing a phone interview.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "Not knowing anything about the company or job you're interviewing for is way more obvious than you'd think. \"Many people think that they don't have to put as much effort into researching the role or the company,\" says Steve Pritchard, an HR consultant. And if you have your laptop in front of you during the interview to do a few quick searches, they won't know the difference, right? Not exactly. \"Seasoned interviewers will know whether an interviewee is researching while on the phone. The interviewer can often even hear the typing as they ask the question,\" he adds."}, {"label": "K", "text": "\"The key to success during a phone interview is clear and concise answers,\" says Dardis. \"People's attention spans tend to be shorter over the phone. You don't want your future employer to lose interest in the conversation.\" He recommends practicing answers to questions you know will be asked ahead of time in order to be clear on what you're going to say. That way, you can prevent talking in an aimless manner."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Disgust is a universal human emotion. One type of disgust is called physical disgust, which protects us from eating or touching things that could be dangerous for us, such as raw meat or insects. Children experience physical disgust from a very early age, but do not achieve an understanding of the concept until they are around four years old.\nSome researchers maintain that there is another type of disgust, called moral disgust, that protects us from engaging in activities that can be perceived as morally wrong. Examples of moral disgust are stealing, lying or cheating.\nHowever, not all academics think that moral disgust really exists. Some say that when we refer to disgust in relation to moral issues, we are using disgust as a metaphor(隐喻), and what we are feeling is in reality anger.\nIf moral disgust appears quite late in development, it makes sense to think that there is actually a social component to it. Children learn about emotions in many different ways. Of special relevance are parents-children conversations about emotions. Indeed, children whose parents talk about emotions often have a better emotional understanding than children whose parents do not discuss emotions often.\nHowever, there are no studies that have examined how parents and children talk about disgust. This is what we set up to examine in our study.\nWe asked 68 English-speaking mothers and their four-, six- or eight-year-old children to discuss some stories about moral and physical disgust. Our preliminary findings show mothers and children do talk about disgust, but mainly about physical disgust and not so much about moral disgust.\nWhen we asked mothers and children to discuss moral issues, such as whether it is OK to play with a child who has stolen someone else's snack, they linked this type of moral wrongdoing with the emotion of anger and not disgust.\nDoes this mean that moral disgust does not exist? Some researchers suggest moral disgust exists in adults. Our findings suggest that moral disgust is either understood at a later age or is only used metaphorically, if at all, in children as old as eight years.", "47": "Disgust is a universal human emotion. One type of disgust is called physical disgust, which protects us from eating or touching things that could be dangerous for us, such as raw meat or insects. Children experience physical disgust from a very early age, but do not achieve an understanding of the concept until they are around four years old.\nSome researchers maintain that there is another type of disgust, called moral disgust, that protects us from engaging in activities that can be perceived as morally wrong. Examples of moral disgust are stealing, lying or cheating.\nHowever, not all academics think that moral disgust really exists. Some say that when we refer to disgust in relation to moral issues, we are using disgust as a metaphor(隐喻), and what we are feeling is in reality anger.\nIf moral disgust appears quite late in development, it makes sense to think that there is actually a social component to it. Children learn about emotions in many different ways. Of special relevance are parents-children conversations about emotions. Indeed, children whose parents talk about emotions often have a better emotional understanding than children whose parents do not discuss emotions often.\nHowever, there are no studies that have examined how parents and children talk about disgust. This is what we set up to examine in our study.\nWe asked 68 English-speaking mothers and their four-, six- or eight-year-old children to discuss some stories about moral and physical disgust. Our preliminary findings show mothers and children do talk about disgust, but mainly about physical disgust and not so much about moral disgust.\nWhen we asked mothers and children to discuss moral issues, such as whether it is OK to play with a child who has stolen someone else's snack, they linked this type of moral wrongdoing with the emotion of anger and not disgust.\nDoes this mean that moral disgust does not exist? Some researchers suggest moral disgust exists in adults. Our findings suggest that moral disgust is either understood at a later age or is only used metaphorically, if at all, in children as old as eight years.", "48": "Disgust is a universal human emotion. One type of disgust is called physical disgust, which protects us from eating or touching things that could be dangerous for us, such as raw meat or insects. Children experience physical disgust from a very early age, but do not achieve an understanding of the concept until they are around four years old.\nSome researchers maintain that there is another type of disgust, called moral disgust, that protects us from engaging in activities that can be perceived as morally wrong. Examples of moral disgust are stealing, lying or cheating.\nHowever, not all academics think that moral disgust really exists. Some say that when we refer to disgust in relation to moral issues, we are using disgust as a metaphor(隐喻), and what we are feeling is in reality anger.\nIf moral disgust appears quite late in development, it makes sense to think that there is actually a social component to it. Children learn about emotions in many different ways. Of special relevance are parents-children conversations about emotions. Indeed, children whose parents talk about emotions often have a better emotional understanding than children whose parents do not discuss emotions often.\nHowever, there are no studies that have examined how parents and children talk about disgust. This is what we set up to examine in our study.\nWe asked 68 English-speaking mothers and their four-, six- or eight-year-old children to discuss some stories about moral and physical disgust. Our preliminary findings show mothers and children do talk about disgust, but mainly about physical disgust and not so much about moral disgust.\nWhen we asked mothers and children to discuss moral issues, such as whether it is OK to play with a child who has stolen someone else's snack, they linked this type of moral wrongdoing with the emotion of anger and not disgust.\nDoes this mean that moral disgust does not exist? Some researchers suggest moral disgust exists in adults. Our findings suggest that moral disgust is either understood at a later age or is only used metaphorically, if at all, in children as old as eight years.", "49": "Disgust is a universal human emotion. One type of disgust is called physical disgust, which protects us from eating or touching things that could be dangerous for us, such as raw meat or insects. Children experience physical disgust from a very early age, but do not achieve an understanding of the concept until they are around four years old.\nSome researchers maintain that there is another type of disgust, called moral disgust, that protects us from engaging in activities that can be perceived as morally wrong. Examples of moral disgust are stealing, lying or cheating.\nHowever, not all academics think that moral disgust really exists. Some say that when we refer to disgust in relation to moral issues, we are using disgust as a metaphor(隐喻), and what we are feeling is in reality anger.\nIf moral disgust appears quite late in development, it makes sense to think that there is actually a social component to it. Children learn about emotions in many different ways. Of special relevance are parents-children conversations about emotions. Indeed, children whose parents talk about emotions often have a better emotional understanding than children whose parents do not discuss emotions often.\nHowever, there are no studies that have examined how parents and children talk about disgust. This is what we set up to examine in our study.\nWe asked 68 English-speaking mothers and their four-, six- or eight-year-old children to discuss some stories about moral and physical disgust. Our preliminary findings show mothers and children do talk about disgust, but mainly about physical disgust and not so much about moral disgust.\nWhen we asked mothers and children to discuss moral issues, such as whether it is OK to play with a child who has stolen someone else's snack, they linked this type of moral wrongdoing with the emotion of anger and not disgust.\nDoes this mean that moral disgust does not exist? Some researchers suggest moral disgust exists in adults. Our findings suggest that moral disgust is either understood at a later age or is only used metaphorically, if at all, in children as old as eight years.", "50": "Disgust is a universal human emotion. One type of disgust is called physical disgust, which protects us from eating or touching things that could be dangerous for us, such as raw meat or insects. Children experience physical disgust from a very early age, but do not achieve an understanding of the concept until they are around four years old.\nSome researchers maintain that there is another type of disgust, called moral disgust, that protects us from engaging in activities that can be perceived as morally wrong. Examples of moral disgust are stealing, lying or cheating.\nHowever, not all academics think that moral disgust really exists. Some say that when we refer to disgust in relation to moral issues, we are using disgust as a metaphor(隐喻), and what we are feeling is in reality anger.\nIf moral disgust appears quite late in development, it makes sense to think that there is actually a social component to it. Children learn about emotions in many different ways. Of special relevance are parents-children conversations about emotions. Indeed, children whose parents talk about emotions often have a better emotional understanding than children whose parents do not discuss emotions often.\nHowever, there are no studies that have examined how parents and children talk about disgust. This is what we set up to examine in our study.\nWe asked 68 English-speaking mothers and their four-, six- or eight-year-old children to discuss some stories about moral and physical disgust. Our preliminary findings show mothers and children do talk about disgust, but mainly about physical disgust and not so much about moral disgust.\nWhen we asked mothers and children to discuss moral issues, such as whether it is OK to play with a child who has stolen someone else's snack, they linked this type of moral wrongdoing with the emotion of anger and not disgust.\nDoes this mean that moral disgust does not exist? Some researchers suggest moral disgust exists in adults. Our findings suggest that moral disgust is either understood at a later age or is only used metaphorically, if at all, in children as old as eight years.", "51": "After finding out details about a stranger, we mistakenly think that they also know about us. As a result, we act more honestly around them, according to a recent study in Nature.\nPast research has found that we tend to assume social relationships are reciprocal(相互的). Most of the time, this assumption is accurate: someone you think of as a friend will usually consider you a friend too. But sometimes our social ties are more one-sided: for example, you might learn something about a stranger who doesn't know you at all. Researchers Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest wondered whether our tendency to believe that social ties are reciprocal could lead us to mistakenly feel that a stranger does actually know us.\nIn a series of lab-based studies, this is exactly what the researchers found. When we know about strangers, we erroneously think they also know about us. But the really interesting finding came when the researchers stepped out of the lab, to conduct a field experiment. The team developed leaflets(传单) containing fairly ordinary information about local community police officers, such as their favourite food, hobbies, or reasons for joining the police force. They then sent out these leaflets to every apartment in a number of housing developments in disadvantaged areas. The officers themselves also dropped off cards to local residents containing similar information.\nTwo months later, the researchers surveyed residents at these housing developments as well as control areas that did not receive leaflets or outreach cards. Residents were asked to imagine that they had committed a crime and how likely it would be that local officers would find out about it. They also rated how well the officers in the area knew them.\nThe team found that residents of the developments that had received the leaflets believed it more likely that local officers would find out about illegal activity than residents of the control areas. Even more strikingly, the team found that immediately after the leaflet distribution, there were fewer criminal complaints and arrests in the areas around the developments that had received the leaflets than around the control developments.", "52": "After finding out details about a stranger, we mistakenly think that they also know about us. As a result, we act more honestly around them, according to a recent study in Nature.\nPast research has found that we tend to assume social relationships are reciprocal(相互的). Most of the time, this assumption is accurate: someone you think of as a friend will usually consider you a friend too. But sometimes our social ties are more one-sided: for example, you might learn something about a stranger who doesn't know you at all. Researchers Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest wondered whether our tendency to believe that social ties are reciprocal could lead us to mistakenly feel that a stranger does actually know us.\nIn a series of lab-based studies, this is exactly what the researchers found. When we know about strangers, we erroneously think they also know about us. But the really interesting finding came when the researchers stepped out of the lab, to conduct a field experiment. The team developed leaflets(传单) containing fairly ordinary information about local community police officers, such as their favourite food, hobbies, or reasons for joining the police force. They then sent out these leaflets to every apartment in a number of housing developments in disadvantaged areas. The officers themselves also dropped off cards to local residents containing similar information.\nTwo months later, the researchers surveyed residents at these housing developments as well as control areas that did not receive leaflets or outreach cards. Residents were asked to imagine that they had committed a crime and how likely it would be that local officers would find out about it. They also rated how well the officers in the area knew them.\nThe team found that residents of the developments that had received the leaflets believed it more likely that local officers would find out about illegal activity than residents of the control areas. Even more strikingly, the team found that immediately after the leaflet distribution, there were fewer criminal complaints and arrests in the areas around the developments that had received the leaflets than around the control developments.", "53": "After finding out details about a stranger, we mistakenly think that they also know about us. As a result, we act more honestly around them, according to a recent study in Nature.\nPast research has found that we tend to assume social relationships are reciprocal(相互的). Most of the time, this assumption is accurate: someone you think of as a friend will usually consider you a friend too. But sometimes our social ties are more one-sided: for example, you might learn something about a stranger who doesn't know you at all. Researchers Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest wondered whether our tendency to believe that social ties are reciprocal could lead us to mistakenly feel that a stranger does actually know us.\nIn a series of lab-based studies, this is exactly what the researchers found. When we know about strangers, we erroneously think they also know about us. But the really interesting finding came when the researchers stepped out of the lab, to conduct a field experiment. The team developed leaflets(传单) containing fairly ordinary information about local community police officers, such as their favourite food, hobbies, or reasons for joining the police force. They then sent out these leaflets to every apartment in a number of housing developments in disadvantaged areas. The officers themselves also dropped off cards to local residents containing similar information.\nTwo months later, the researchers surveyed residents at these housing developments as well as control areas that did not receive leaflets or outreach cards. Residents were asked to imagine that they had committed a crime and how likely it would be that local officers would find out about it. They also rated how well the officers in the area knew them.\nThe team found that residents of the developments that had received the leaflets believed it more likely that local officers would find out about illegal activity than residents of the control areas. Even more strikingly, the team found that immediately after the leaflet distribution, there were fewer criminal complaints and arrests in the areas around the developments that had received the leaflets than around the control developments.", "54": "After finding out details about a stranger, we mistakenly think that they also know about us. As a result, we act more honestly around them, according to a recent study in Nature.\nPast research has found that we tend to assume social relationships are reciprocal(相互的). Most of the time, this assumption is accurate: someone you think of as a friend will usually consider you a friend too. But sometimes our social ties are more one-sided: for example, you might learn something about a stranger who doesn't know you at all. Researchers Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest wondered whether our tendency to believe that social ties are reciprocal could lead us to mistakenly feel that a stranger does actually know us.\nIn a series of lab-based studies, this is exactly what the researchers found. When we know about strangers, we erroneously think they also know about us. But the really interesting finding came when the researchers stepped out of the lab, to conduct a field experiment. The team developed leaflets(传单) containing fairly ordinary information about local community police officers, such as their favourite food, hobbies, or reasons for joining the police force. They then sent out these leaflets to every apartment in a number of housing developments in disadvantaged areas. The officers themselves also dropped off cards to local residents containing similar information.\nTwo months later, the researchers surveyed residents at these housing developments as well as control areas that did not receive leaflets or outreach cards. Residents were asked to imagine that they had committed a crime and how likely it would be that local officers would find out about it. They also rated how well the officers in the area knew them.\nThe team found that residents of the developments that had received the leaflets believed it more likely that local officers would find out about illegal activity than residents of the control areas. Even more strikingly, the team found that immediately after the leaflet distribution, there were fewer criminal complaints and arrests in the areas around the developments that had received the leaflets than around the control developments.", "55": "After finding out details about a stranger, we mistakenly think that they also know about us. As a result, we act more honestly around them, according to a recent study in Nature.\nPast research has found that we tend to assume social relationships are reciprocal(相互的). Most of the time, this assumption is accurate: someone you think of as a friend will usually consider you a friend too. But sometimes our social ties are more one-sided: for example, you might learn something about a stranger who doesn't know you at all. Researchers Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest wondered whether our tendency to believe that social ties are reciprocal could lead us to mistakenly feel that a stranger does actually know us.\nIn a series of lab-based studies, this is exactly what the researchers found. When we know about strangers, we erroneously think they also know about us. But the really interesting finding came when the researchers stepped out of the lab, to conduct a field experiment. The team developed leaflets(传单) containing fairly ordinary information about local community police officers, such as their favourite food, hobbies, or reasons for joining the police force. They then sent out these leaflets to every apartment in a number of housing developments in disadvantaged areas. The officers themselves also dropped off cards to local residents containing similar information.\nTwo months later, the researchers surveyed residents at these housing developments as well as control areas that did not receive leaflets or outreach cards. Residents were asked to imagine that they had committed a crime and how likely it would be that local officers would find out about it. They also rated how well the officers in the area knew them.\nThe team found that residents of the developments that had received the leaflets believed it more likely that local officers would find out about illegal activity than residents of the control areas. Even more strikingly, the team found that immediately after the leaflet distribution, there were fewer criminal complaints and arrests in the areas around the developments that had received the leaflets than around the control developments."}, "questions": [{"id": "2026-06-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) awareness", "B) canceling", "C) controversial", "D) emptiness", "E) monstrous", "F) offers", "G) parallels", "H) permanently", "I) physical", "J) prepare", "K) prospects", "L) quitting", "M) shed", "N) significantly", "O) trigger"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) physical\n形容词\n【词性槽】与 psychological 并列修饰 symptoms，需形容词\n【依据】physical 与 psychological 构成「身与心」对举\nphysical（身体的）与 psychological symptoms（心理症状）正是「身心」对举；且空后\n例证 such as mood swings, anxiety, headaches and poor sleep 中的 headaches、\npoor sleep 就是身体症状，例证直接印证答案。\n【竞争词】C) controversial 评价性形容词与 psychological 不在同一维度\ncontroversial（有争议的）是对事物的评价，与 psychological 不在同一分类维度，无法并列修\n饰 symptoms；它真正对应的是 __(31)__ 的 a __(31)__ subject。\nE) monstrous 「骇人的」色彩过强、原文无依据\nmonstrous（巨大的/骇人的）程度与感情色彩都过重，原文只是客观描述症状，没有任何「骇\n人」的依据。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) awareness", "B) canceling", "C) controversial", "D) emptiness", "E) monstrous", "F) offers", "G) parallels", "H) permanently", "I) physical", "J) prepare", "K) prospects", "L) quitting", "M) shed", "N) significantly", "O) trigger"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) quitting\n动词\n【词性槽】those 的后置定语，与 using 并列的现在分词\n【依据】quit smoking 戒烟，固定搭配\nquitting 与空后 smoking cigarettes 构成 quit smoking（戒烟）这一近乎绑死的搭配：\nthose __(27)__ smoking cigarettes or using drugs 指「戒烟或戒毒的人」，正好对应全\n文「戒断」主题。\n【竞争词】B) canceling cancel 接「安排」不接「习惯」\ncanceling 意为「取消」，宾语是会议、订单、计划等安排好的事；烟瘾是「戒掉」不是「取\n消」，cancel smoking 不是搭配。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 B) —— canceling 词性、词义都沾边。canceling 与 quitting 同为\n-ing、都含「停止」义，是本卷最阴的干扰。防坑：盯住空后宾语——quit smoking 是固\n定搭配，cancel 不接习惯类宾语。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) awareness", "B) canceling", "C) controversial", "D) emptiness", "E) monstrous", "F) offers", "G) parallels", "H) permanently", "I) physical", "J) prepare", "K) prospects", "L) quitting", "M) shed", "N) significantly", "O) trigger"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) offers\n动词\n【词性槽】单数主语后缺谓语，需三单动词\n【依据】offer evidence 提供证据，研究报道标配\noffers 意为「提供」：The new study __(28)__ the first evidence 句意「这项新研究提\n供了首个证据」，study + offer + evidence 是学术报道高频搭配，that 从句正是证据的\n内容。\n【竞争词】G) parallels 「研究平行于证据」主宾逻辑不成立\nparallels 作动词意为「与……相似/平行」，其主语和宾语应是两个可比较的事物；study\nparallels the evidence（研究相似于证据）逻辑不通。它真正的归宿是 __(30)__。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) awareness", "B) canceling", "C) controversial", "D) emptiness", "E) monstrous", "F) offers", "G) parallels", "H) permanently", "I) physical", "J) prepare", "K) prospects", "L) quitting", "M) shed", "N) significantly", "O) trigger"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) significantly\n副词\n【词性槽】空在比较级 more intense 前，修饰程度，只能副词\n【依据】significantly more 表「显著更」，研究报告程度的标准语气\nsignificantly（显著地）修饰 more intense，正是研究报告里描述程度差异的标配表达：\nwithdrawal symptoms were __(29)__ more intense between the second and fifth\ndays 句意「戒断症状在第 2 到第 5 天显著更强烈」，与有限时间段完全自洽。\n【竞争词】H) permanently 「永久地」与有限时间段自相矛盾\npermanently 意为「永久地」，表时间长度而不表程度强弱；原文 between the second and\nfifth days 明确给出一个有限的时间段，「永久地更强烈」既与时间范围打架，也接不了比较级\nmore intense。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— 副词二选一的抛硬币陷阱。permanently 是全卷唯二副\n词，只看词性不读句意的考生会在这里抛硬币。防坑：副词要连同被修饰成分和句中时间\n词一起读——between the second and fifth days 与「永久」矛盾，significantly 才表\n程度。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) awareness", "B) canceling", "C) controversial", "D) emptiness", "E) monstrous", "F) offers", "G) parallels", "H) permanently", "I) physical", "J) prepare", "K) prospects", "L) quitting", "M) shed", "N) significantly", "O) trigger"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) parallels\n动词\n【词性槽】which 定语从句缺谓语，需三单动词\n【依据】parallels 表「与……相平行/对应」，是 similar to 的动词版\nparallels 句意「（第 2~5 天最强）这与人们戒毒时熬过来的时间跨度相平行/对应」，呼应\n首段 symptoms similar to drug withdrawal 的「相似于戒毒」主线——parallels 正是\nsimilar to 的动词说法。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) awareness", "B) canceling", "C) controversial", "D) emptiness", "E) monstrous", "F) offers", "G) parallels", "H) permanently", "I) physical", "J) prepare", "K) prospects", "L) quitting", "M) shed", "N) significantly", "O) trigger"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) controversial\n形容词\n【词性槽】冠词 a 后、名词 subject 前，典型定语位需形容词\n【依据】「有争议的话题」，与 Although 让步转折呼应\ncontroversial 修饰 subject（话题）：后文 Although prior studies in animals and\nhumans have shown some biological and behavioral similarities between substance-\nuse disorders and addictive-like consumption of highly processed foods, no studies\nhave looked at whether reducing junk food can __(32)__ withdrawal symptoms in\npeople 的让步转折正说明这个话题「有争议」——有相似证据，却无直接研究。\n【竞争词】E) monstrous 「骇人的话题」色彩与学术语气不符\nmonstrous 意为「骇人的/巨大的」，学术报道里不会说一个 subject「骇人」；全文语气客\n观，monster 的强烈色彩无处安放。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) awareness", "B) canceling", "C) controversial", "D) emptiness", "E) monstrous", "F) offers", "G) parallels", "H) permanently", "I) physical", "J) prepare", "K) prospects", "L) quitting", "M) shed", "N) significantly", "O) trigger"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) trigger\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 can 后接原形及物动词\n【依据】trigger withdrawal symptoms 引发戒断症状\ntrigger 意为「引发、触发」，trigger + 症状是医学心理语境的常见搭配：whether\nreducing junk food can __(32)__ withdrawal symptoms in people 句意「减少吃垃圾食\n品是否会引发戒断症状」，正是首段 experience symptoms similar to drug withdrawal\n提出的研究问题。\n【竞争词】J) prepare 「准备症状」语义荒谬\nprepare 是「准备」，其宾语应是计划、策略、餐食等人可支配之物；prepare withdrawal\nsymptoms（准备戒断症状）逻辑不通——没有人去「准备」症状。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) awareness", "B) canceling", "C) controversial", "D) emptiness", "E) monstrous", "F) offers", "G) parallels", "H) permanently", "I) physical", "J) prepare", "K) prospects", "L) quitting", "M) shed", "N) significantly", "O) trigger"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) awareness\n名词\n【词性槽】及物动词 Raising 后缺宾语，且带 that 同位语从句，需名词\n【依据】raise awareness 提高认识，固定搭配\nawareness 与句首 Raising 构成 raise awareness（提高认识）这一高频固定搭配，that 从\n句正是「认识」的内容：人们戒掉垃圾食品时可能烦躁或头痛——提高这种认识，才能帮助\n下文的 prepare coping strategies。\n【竞争词】D) emptiness 「提高空虚」语义荒谬\nemptiness（空虚）作 raise 的宾语不成语义——「提高空虚感」既不是搭配，也与「帮助人们\n提前准备应对策略」的积极目的相悖。\nK) prospects prospects 接不了 that 同位语从句\nprospects（前景）虽能勉强接 raise，但 prospects 后接 that 同位语从句很别扭——that 从句\n的内容（人会烦躁头痛）无法说明「前景」；而 awareness that……（意识到……）天衣无缝。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) awareness", "B) canceling", "C) controversial", "D) emptiness", "E) monstrous", "F) offers", "G) parallels", "H) permanently", "I) physical", "J) prepare", "K) prospects", "L) quitting", "M) shed", "N) significantly", "O) trigger"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) prepare\n动词\n【词性槽】help sb (to) do 结构中接原形动词\n【依据】prepare coping strategies 提前准备应对策略\nprepare 与宾语 coping strategies 搭配自然：help individuals __(34)__ coping\nstrategies in advance 句意「帮助人们提前准备好应对策略」，句末的 in advance 与\nprepare 的「提前」语义恰好呼应。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) awareness", "B) canceling", "C) controversial", "D) emptiness", "E) monstrous", "F) offers", "G) parallels", "H) permanently", "I) physical", "J) prepare", "K) prospects", "L) quitting", "M) shed", "N) significantly", "O) trigger"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) shed\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 may 后接原形动词，且与 light on 构成动宾框架\n【依据】shed light on 是固定搭配「阐明」\nshed 与空后的 light on the barriers 构成固定短语 shed light on（阐明、使清楚）：「这\n些发现也可以阐明人们改变饮食习惯时面对的障碍」，与全文「戒断症状是改变饮食习惯的\n障碍」的主旨完全贴合。\n【竞争词】J) prepare prepare light on 搭配不成立\nprepare 后接名词表「准备某物」，prepare light on the barriers 既不成语义、也不是任何固\n定搭配。\nO) trigger trigger 接后果类宾语，不接 light on\ntrigger 意为「引发」，宾语多为症状、反应、危机等后果类名词；trigger light 不是搭配，\n「引发光」语义不通。它真正的归宿是 __(32)__ 的 trigger withdrawal symptoms。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Interviewees are advised not to ask questions about pay during a phone interview.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】questions about pay\n【定位】The question regarding pay is also best avoided. To be frank, it's simply too early in the\nprocess for you to be the one who brings up salary expectations. \"Chances are if a candidate\nis participating in a phone interview, this is the first time they have talked with the company,\nand the first call isn't the appropriate time to talk about 'what's in it for you,'\" says Justina\nStrnad, the Talent Acquisition Manager for Shiftgig.\n关于薪资的问题也最好避开。坦率地说，现在就让求职者主动提出薪资期望还为时过早。'如\n果候选人正在参加电话面试，这很可能是他们第一次与公司交谈，第一次通话并不是讨论'这\n对你有什么好处'的合适时机，'Shiftgig的人才招聘经理Justina Strnad说。\n【改写】not ask questions about pay ↔ The question regarding pay is also best avoided\n同义改写：not ask → best avoided\nduring a phone interview ↔ if a candidate is participating in a phone interview 与 the first call\n场景锁定：电话面试初次通话", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Experienced interviewers will know if an applicant is searching for information online while talking on the phone.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Experienced interviewers / searching online\n【定位】\"Seasoned interviewers will know whether an interviewee is researching while on the phone.\nThe interviewer can often even hear the typing as they ask the question,\" he adds.\n经验丰富的面试官会知道应聘者是否在通话时查资料。面试官甚至常常能在提问时听到打字\n声，'他补充道。\n【改写】Experienced interviewers ↔ Seasoned interviewers\n同义词替换\nsearching for information online ↔ researching while on the phone\n同义短语 + 场景化\nwill know if an applicant is searching for information ↔\nwill know whether an interviewee is researching\n句式压缩\n【辨邻】 A prepared with as much information as possible 强调面试前要主动搜集信息；而\n题干说的是'通话中在线搜索'，这是 researching while on the phone，两段时间点\n不同。\ncandidates are expected to go into them prepared with as much information as possible about the\ncompany, position, and their own skills and strengths", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Many employers regard the phone interview as the first step of the official recruiting process.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】first step of the official recruiting process\n【定位】Many companies treat phone screening as the official first round of the hiring process.\n许多公司把电话筛选视为招聘流程的正式第一轮。\n【改写】Many employers ↔ Many companies\n同义替换\nthe phone interview ↔ phone screening\n同义替换 / 上下位\nthe first step of the official recruiting process ↔ the official first round of the hiring process\n同义改写：step→round, recruiting→hiring", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Applicants who don't ask questions at the end of an interview most probably ruin their chance of getting the position.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】don't ask questions at the end\n【定位】\"If we have had a great phone interview and then we wrap up and they don't have any\nquestions for me, it pretty much ruins the whole interview. It tells me that the candidate is\nuninterested in the role, which in reality might not be the case at all,\" she notes.\n如果我们进行了一次很棒的电话面试，然后结束收尾时他们对我没有任何问题，那基本上就\n毁了整场面试。这告诉我候选人对这个职位不感兴趣，而现实中可能根本不是这样，'她说。\n【改写】don't ask questions at the end of an interview ↔\nwrap up and they don't have any questions for me\n场景 + 否定对应\nmost probably ruin their chance of getting the position ↔\nit pretty much ruins the whole interview 与 the candidate is uninterested in the role\n结果对应 + 因果展开", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "The interviewee should test their phone connection before they call the interviewer.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】test their phone connection\n【定位】\"Make sure you test your headset and connection before dialing in,\" recommends Roark.\n\"There is nothing more frustrating for a recruiter who has a structured interview guide in\nplace having to repeatedly ask the same question over and over because they could not\nunderstand your answer due to dropped signals.\" Test-call a friend beforehand, or even call\nyourself from a landline if necessary; it will take less than a minute.\n罗克建议：'务必在拨入前测试你的耳机和连接。对于已经准备好结构化面试指南的招聘人员\n来说，没有什么比因为信号中断而反复问同一个问题更令人沮丧的了。'提前给朋友打个测试\n电话，必要时甚至用座机给自己打；这花不到一分钟。\n【改写】The interviewee should test their phone connection ↔\nMake sure you test your headset and connection\n同义改写：phone connection → headset and connection\nbefore they call the interviewer ↔ before dialing in\n同义替换：call → dial in", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "It is annoying to the interviewer to find the applicant doing other tasks while being interviewed.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】doing other tasks\n【定位】It might be tempting to cross something off your to-do list while on a phone interview, but\nrecruiters and hiring managers can easily tell if your attention is elsewhere. Many\ninterviewers are annoyed to see people who decide to multitask while on the phone interview.\nThere are candidates who wash dishes, go for walks, or do grocery shopping during\nthe interview.\n在电话面试时顺便划掉待办事项可能很诱人，但招聘人员和用人经理很容易看出你的注意力\n是否在别处。许多面试官对有人在电话面试时决定一心多用感到恼火。有候选人在面试时洗\n碗、散步或采购杂货。\n【改写】doing other tasks while being interviewed ↔\nmultitask while on the phone interview 与 wash dishes, go for walks, or do grocery shopping\nduring the interview\n概括 → 具体化\nannoying to the interviewer ↔ Many interviewers are annoyed\n情感态度对应", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "To pass a phone interview, the most important thing is to answer questions clearly and concisely.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】answer questions clearly and concisely\n【定位】\"The key to success during a phone interview is clear and concise answers,\" says Dardis.\n\"People's attention spans tend to be shorter over the phone. You don't want your future\nemployer to lose interest in the conversation.\" He recommends practicing answers to\nquestions you know will be asked ahead of time in order to be clear on what you're going to\nsay. That way, you can prevent talking in an aimless manner.\n达迪斯说：'电话面试成功的关键是清晰简洁的回答。人们在电话中的注意力往往更短。你不\n想让未来雇主对谈话失去兴趣。'他建议提前练习那些你知道会被问到的问题，以便清楚自己\n要说什么。这样你就能避免漫无目的地说话。\n【改写】To pass a phone interview ↔ success during a phone interview\n同义替换：pass → success\nthe most important thing is to answer questions clearly and concisely ↔\nThe key to success during a phone interview is clear and concise answers\n同义改写：most important thing → key, clearly and concisely → clear and concise\n【辨邻】 H interrupting the interviewer is awkward and rude 与 speaking too fast, speaking\ntoo loud 都涉及电话中的口头表现，但讲的是礼貌与语速音量；题干问的是回答要'清\n晰简洁'，这是 clear and concise answers 的内容。\ninterrupting the interviewer is awkward and rude", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "If you fail to call the interviewer on time, they might not see you as a suitable candidate.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】call the interviewer on time\n【定位】\"About a quarter of the people with whom I schedule phone interviews aren't on time,\" says\nSophie Cikovsky, who handles U.S. recruiting for Infinite Global. \"While this bothers me\npersonally, it's also indicative of someone who isn't very detail-oriented,\" she explains. \"In\norder to identify this early in the hiring process, I started asking all candidates a few years ago\nto call me as opposed to calling them at an agreed-upon time. That way, if I hear from them at\n1:13 p.m. or 12:49 p.m. instead of our planned 1:00 p.m. interview time, I have an early\nindicator that they might not be a great fit.\"\n无限环球美国招聘负责人索菲·西科夫斯基说：'我安排电话面试的人中大约有四分之一不准\n时。虽然这让我个人很烦，但也说明这个人不太注重细节。为了在招聘早期识别这一点，几\n年前我开始让所有候选人给我打电话，而不是我在约定时间给他们打。这样，如果他们在下\n午1:13或12:49联系我，而不是我们约定的下午1:00，我就有一个早期指标表明他们可能不\n太合适。'\n【改写】fail to call the interviewer on time ↔\naren't on time 与 call me as opposed to calling them at an agreed-upon time\n同义改写 + 主被动转换\nthey might not see you as a suitable candidate ↔ might not be a great fit\n同义替换：suitable candidate → great fit", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Applicants should avoid talking about their personal lives if the interviewer has only asked about their professional background and goals.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】personal lives / professional background and goals\n【定位】\"The point of a phone interview is to focus on getting to know a candidate's professional\nexperience and goals,\" says Mckenzie Roark, campus talent specialist at Lithko Contracting.\n\"A recruiter is trying to see if they are the best fit for a role, and learning about their personal\nlife doesn't help. For example, when asked where you see yourself in five years, we don't\nwant to know that you hope to be married or that you want to buy a new house. That is nice,\nbut that isn't relative to anything professional.\"\nLithko Contracting校园人才专家麦肯齐·罗克说：'电话面试的重点是了解候选人的职业经历\n和目标。招聘人员想判断他们是否最适合该职位，而了解个人生活没有帮助。例如，当被问\n到五年后你看到自己在哪里时，我们不想知道你想结婚或想买新房。这很好，但与任何职业\n内容无关。'\n【改写】avoid talking about their personal lives ↔\nDon't talk about your personal life 与 learning about their personal life doesn't help\n同义改写：avoid → don't talk\nif the interviewer has only asked about their professional background and goals ↔\nfocus on getting to know a candidate's professional experience and goals\n同义替换：background → experience", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "It is impolite for an interviewee to interrupt the interviewer in the phone conversation.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】interrupt the interviewer\n【定位】You might be eager to get your point across or talk about your experience, but interrupting the\ninterviewer is awkward and rude. \"Interviewing can be stressful, and sometimes that stress\nmanifests itself in speaking too fast, speaking too loud, talking over the interviewer, or\nattempting to answer the interviewer's question before they have actually finished asking the\nquestion,\" says Chris Dardis, a recruiting expert. \"Don't do this.\" There's a big difference\nbetween being assertive(自信的) and being aggressive, and interviewers can always\nrecognize it.\n你可能急于表达观点或谈论经验，但打断面试官既尴尬又粗鲁。招聘专家克里斯·达迪斯\n说：'面试可能有压力，有时这种压力表现为说话太快、太大声、打断面试官，或在面试官还\n没问完问题时就试图回答。不要这样做。'自信与咄咄逼人之间有很大区别，面试官总能识别\n出来。\n【改写】It is impolite for an interviewee to interrupt the interviewer ↔\ninterrupting the interviewer is awkward and rude\n同义改写：impolite → rude\nin the phone conversation ↔ Interviewing can be stressful... talking over the interviewer\n场景展开\n【辨邻】 K clear and concise answers 与 prevent talking in an aimless manner 都涉及口头表\n达质量，但主题是内容简洁；题干问的是'打断面试官不礼貌'，这是 interrupting the\ninterviewer is awkward and rude。\nThe key to success during a phone interview is clear and concise answers", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the passage tell us about physical disgust?", "options": ["A) It is an emotion as contrasted with thoughts.", "B) It is a concept beyond children's understanding.", "C) It is a mechanism to keep us from being harmed.", "D) It is an experience gained from any form of danger."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage tell us about physical disgust?是在问文章对physical disgust的\n客观说明，不涉及推理、态度或主旨。\n【拆句】What does the passage tell us about physical disgust?是主谓宾结构的特殊疑问句，主\n语the passage，谓语tell，间接宾语us，直接宾语由what充当，about physical disgust限\n定话题范围，无否定、比较等陷阱。\n【定位】One type of disgust is called physical disgust, which protects us from eating or touching\nthings that could be dangerous for us, such as raw meat or insects.\n厌恶感的一种类型被称为生理厌恶，它保护我们不去吃或触碰可能对我们有危险的东西，比\n如生肉或昆虫。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nIt is an emotion as contrasted with thoughts.在原文Disgust is a universal human\nemotion.基础上添加了未出现的\"as contrasted with thoughts\"，把客观陈述偷换成对\n立关系。\nB) 与原文相反\nIt is a concept beyond children's understanding.截取了do not achieve an\nunderstanding of the concept until they are around four years old中的否定，却忽\n略\"until around four\"，将\"暂时未理解\"反转为\"永远无法理解\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nphysical disgust, which protects us from eating or touching things that could be dangerous for us\n↔ It is a mechanism to keep us from being harmed.\nIt is a mechanism to keep us from being harmed.与protects us from eating or\ntouching things that could be dangerous for us形成语法层面的同义改写，是最佳\n选项。\nD) 偷换概念\nIt is an experience gained from any form of danger.将定位句中的\"protects us\nfrom eating or touching things that could be dangerous\"改为\"experience gained\nfrom danger\"，把防御机制篡改为经验来源，且any form of danger绝对化。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "How do some researchers perceive moral disgust?", "options": ["A) It tends to appear in various forms of wrongdoing.", "B) It is just as annoying to people as physical disgust.", "C) It is a term used by common folk to describe anger.", "D) It stops people from doing anything morally wrong."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】How do some researchers perceive moral disgust?是在问some researchers对moral\ndisgust的具体看法，属于文中可直接找到的事实。\n【拆句】How do some researchers perceive moral disgust?是特殊疑问句，主语some\nresearchers，谓语perceive，宾语moral disgust；how询问方式/看法，整体结构平直。\n【定位】Some researchers maintain that there is another type of disgust, called moral disgust, that\nprotects us from engaging in activities that can be perceived as morally wrong.\n一些研究者认为还存在另一种厌恶，称为道德厌恶，它保护我们不去做那些可能被视为道德\n错误的行为。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nIt tends to appear in various forms of wrongdoing.把Examples of moral disgust\nare stealing, lying or cheating.中的举例包装成\"moral disgust本身以多种形式出现\"，\n混淆了\"触发它的行为\"与\"它本身\"。\nB) 无中生有\nIt is just as annoying to people as physical disgust.使用just as...as结构，但原文从\n未比较两种厌恶的annoying程度，也未评价其是否annoying。\nC) 张冠李戴\nIt is a term used by common folk to describe anger.源于Some say that when we\nrefer to disgust in relation to moral issues, we are using disgust as a metaphor(隐\n喻), and what we are feeling is in reality anger.，但该观点是some academics而非\nsome researchers，且common folk缩小了原句we的范围。\nD) ✓ 正确\nprotects us from engaging in activities that can be perceived as morally wrong ↔\nIt stops people from doing anything morally wrong.\nIt stops people from doing anything morally wrong.与protects us from engaging\nin activities that can be perceived as morally wrong.在语义场和语法结构上均对应，\n是正确项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "Why does moral disgust appear quite late in children's development?", "options": ["A) It needs to be learnt through social interactions.", "B) They have little control over its learning process.", "C) It is too complex for them to acquire.", "D) Their parents seldom talk about emotions."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题）\n【判型】Why does moral disgust appear quite late in children's development?不是直接引用某\n个事实句，而是要求从文中因果解释中推进一步。\n【拆句】Why does moral disgust appear quite late in children's development?是why引导的特\n殊疑问句，主语moral disgust，谓语appear，状语quite late in children's\ndevelopment；询问原因，需在定位句附近找因果解释。\n【定位】If moral disgust appears quite late in development, it makes sense to think that there is\nactually a social component to it. Children learn about emotions in many different ways.\n如果道德厌恶在成长过程中出现得相当晚，那么认为它实际上具有社会成分是合理的。儿童\n以多种方式学习情绪。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nsocial component to it. Children learn about emotions in many different ways ↔\nIt needs to be learnt through social interactions.\nIt needs to be learnt through social interactions.是原文因果链的一步推理，既未跳\n跃也未添加新信息。\nB) 无中生有\nThey have little control over its learning process.引入control概念，但原文Children\nlearn about emotions in many different ways只说学习方式多，未涉及控制程度。\nC) 过度推断\nIt is too complex for them to acquire.用too...to结构把原文的\"late\"和\"social\ncomponent\"臆断为复杂度问题，原文未出现complex或acquire difficulty。\nD) 与原文相反\nTheir parents seldom talk about emotions.与children whose parents talk about\nemotions often have a better emotional understanding than children whose\nparents do not discuss emotions often.直接相反，原文强调父母经常谈情绪。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What did the author and his colleagues want to examine in their experiment?", "options": ["A) What parents tell children against moral wrongdoing.", "B) What parents and children link moral wrongdoing to.", "C) How moral disgust is seen by children aged 4, 6 and 8.", "D) How disgust is discussed between parents and children."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What did the author and his colleagues want to examine in their experiment?直接询\n问研究目的，文中有明确对应句。\n【拆句】What did the author and his colleagues want to examine in their experiment?是what\n引导的特殊疑问句，主语the author and his colleagues，谓语want to examine，in\ntheir experiment限定范围；did提示过去时，对应文中已完成的研究。\n【定位】However, there are no studies that have examined how parents and children talk about\ndisgust. This is what we set up to examine in our study.\n然而，还没有研究考察过父母和孩子如何谈论厌恶。这正是我们研究要考察的问题。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nWhat parents tell children against moral wrongdoing.把how parents and children\ntalk about disgust中的talk about disgust替换为tell children against moral\nwrongdoing，范围收窄且改变了话题。\nB) 答非所问\nWhat parents and children link moral wrongdoing to.对应的是they linked this\ntype of moral wrongdoing with the emotion of anger and not disgust.，这是实验\n结果，不是作者想考察的问题。\nC) 偷换概念\nHow moral disgust is seen by children aged 4, 6 and 8.把four-, six- or eight-year-\nold children中的年龄信息放大为研究核心，但原文研究的是父母与孩子如何谈\ndisgust，而非儿童如何看待moral disgust。\nD) ✓ 正确\nhow parents and children talk about disgust ↔\nHow disgust is discussed between parents and children.\nHow disgust is discussed between parents and children.与how parents and\nchildren talk about disgust. This is what we set up to examine in our study.逐成分\n对应，完全等值。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What can we infer about moral disgust from the author and his colleagues' findings?", "options": ["A) It is a notion hardly comprehensible to children.", "B) It rarely exists in children younger than eight.", "C) It is a term most parents use when talking to children.", "D) It exists in adults with a strong sense of justice."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题）\n【判型】What can we infer about moral disgust from the author and his colleagues'\nfindings?要求从研究发现中概括出隐含结论。\n【拆句】What can we infer about moral disgust from the author and his colleagues'\nfindings?是what引导的特殊疑问句，can infer表示推断，about moral disgust限定对\n象，from the author and his colleagues' findings限定推理依据；需要在结论句基础上做\n一步概括。\n【定位】Our findings suggest that moral disgust is either understood at a later age or is only used\nmetaphorically, if at all, in children as old as eight years.\n我们的发现表明，道德厌恶要么在更晚的年龄才能被理解，要么即便存在，在八岁儿童中也\n只被隐喻式地使用。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\neither understood at a later age or is only used metaphorically, if at all, in children as old as eight\nyears\n↔ It is a notion hardly comprehensible to children.\nIt is a notion hardly comprehensible to children.是结论句的一步同义转述，未过度\n延伸。\nB) 偷换概念\nIt rarely exists in children younger than eight.把only used metaphorically, if at all\n中的使用方式篡改为存在频率，且younger than eight与原文as old as eight不一致。\nC) 与原文相反\nIt is a term most parents use when talking to children.与do talk about disgust,\nbut mainly about physical disgust and not so much about moral disgust.直接相\n反，父母并不常使用moral disgust一词。\nD) 无中生有\nIt exists in adults with a strong sense of justice.虽然提到Some researchers\nsuggest moral disgust exists in adults.，但\"with a strong sense of justice\"是原文\n没有的信息。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What is the finding of a recent study in Nature?", "options": ["A) People are more honest with those who know them than with total strangers.", "B) People we want to have detailed information about are keen to know about us.", "C) People who get to know about a person wrongly assume the person knows them too.", "D) People who are familiar with each other tend to behave honestly in their everyday dealings."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is the finding of a recent study in Nature? 直接要求提取 recent study in Nature\n的具体结论，不涉及全文主旨或作者态度。\n【拆句】What is the finding of a recent study in Nature? 是主系表结构的特殊疑问句，主语为\nthe finding of a recent study in Nature，结构平铺直叙，无隐藏否定或比较陷阱。\n【定位】After finding out details about a stranger, we mistakenly think that they also know about us.\n在了解到一个陌生人的一些细节后，我们错误地认为他们也了解我们。\n【选项】A) 比较结构警惕对象与方向\nPeople are more honest with those who know them than with total strangers. 把\n原文的诚实对象从陌生人偷换成熟人，并将比较对象反向设置。\nB) 无中生有\nPeople we want to have detailed information about are keen to know about us.\n中的 want 与 keen 是原文没有的主观意愿，属于无中生有。\nC) ✓ 正确\nAfter finding out details about a stranger ↔ People who get to know about a person\nwe mistakenly think that they also know about us ↔\nwrongly assume the person knows them too\nPeople who get to know about a person wrongly assume the person knows\nthem too. 与 After finding out details about a stranger, we mistakenly think that\nthey also know about us. 在语义上完全等价，仅做了代词和词汇替换。\nD) 偷换概念\nPeople who are familiar with each other tend to behave honestly in their\neveryday dealings. 将原文的 stranger 替换为 familiar with each other，把研究对象\n从陌生人之间的错觉改为熟人之间的日常行为。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does the passage say about the assumption that social relationships are reciprocal?", "options": ["A) It is more the exception than the rule.", "B) It does not hold true in every situation.", "C) It does not apply to complex relationships.", "D) It is more accurate in one-sided social ties."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say about the assumption... 要求回到原文找关于该\nassumption 的具体描述，属于局部信息提取。\n【拆句】What does the passage say about the assumption that social relationships are\nreciprocal? 中 that 引导同位语从句修饰 assumption，整体是主谓宾结构，无复杂从句\n嵌套。\n【定位】Past research has found that we tend to assume social relationships are reciprocal. Most of the\ntime, this assumption is accurate: someone you think of as a friend will usually consider you\na friend too. But sometimes our social ties are more one-sided: for example, you might learn\nsomething about a stranger who doesn't know you at all.\n过去的研究发现，我们倾向于认为社会关系是相互的。大多数时候这一假设是准确的：你视\n为朋友的人通常也视你为朋友。但有时社会关系更单向：例如，你可能了解到一个根本不认\n识你的陌生人的某些事情。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt is more the exception than the rule. 表示互惠假设多为例外，但原文说 Most of\nthe time 是准确的，因此该选项与原文相反。\nB) ✓ 正确\nMost of the time, this assumption is accurate: someone you think of as a friend will usually\nconsider you a friend too. But sometimes our social ties are more one-sided\n↔ It does not hold true in every situation.\nIt does not hold true in every situation. 没有绝对化，也没有扩大范围，恰好对应\nMost of the time... But sometimes... 的语义。\nC) 无中生有\nIt does not apply to complex relationships. 提到复杂关系，但原文只讨论 one-\nsided ties，并未对关系复杂程度分类。\nD) 与原文相反\nIt is more accurate in one-sided social ties. 与原文相反，原文指出 one-sided ties\n正是假设不准确的例外情况。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What did Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest want to find out about the belief that social relationships are reciprocal?", "options": ["A) Whether it may resolve misunderstandings about social ties.", "B) Whether it could help develop one's relationships with strangers.", "C) Whether it could help strengthen interpersonal relationships.", "D) Whether it may lead to false assumptions about a stranger."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What did Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest want to find out... 聚焦于研究者的研究意\n图，属于局部细节。\n【拆句】What did Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest want to find out about the belief that\nsocial relationships are reciprocal? 中 that social relationships are reciprocal 为同位语\n从句，修饰 belief，整体询问过去的研究目的。\n【定位】Researchers Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest wondered whether our tendency to believe that\nsocial relationships are reciprocal could lead us to mistakenly feel that a stranger does\nactually know us.\n研究者 Anuj Shah 和 Michael LaForest 想知道，我们相信社会关系是互惠的这种倾向，是\n否会让我们误以为一个陌生人确实认识我们。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nWhether it may resolve misunderstandings about social ties. 用 resolve\nmisunderstandings 替换了原文的 lead to mistakenly feel，方向完全相反。\nB) 无中生有\nWhether it could help develop one's relationships with strangers. 中的 develop\nrelationships 是原文没有的积极目的，属于无中生有。\nC) 偷换概念\nWhether it could help strengthen interpersonal relationships. 同样引入\nstrengthen interpersonal relationships 这一积极结果，偏离原文的研究问题。\nD) ✓ 正确\nour tendency to believe that social relationships are reciprocal could lead us to mistakenly feel\nthat a stranger does actually know us\n↔ Whether it may lead to false assumptions about a stranger.\nWhether it may lead to false assumptions about a stranger. 中的 it 指代 the belief\nthat social relationships are reciprocal，lead to false assumptions 对应原文的 lead\nus to mistakenly feel，是对研究问题的精确概括。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "When did Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest find something really interesting in their research?", "options": ["A) During lab-based studies.", "B) During a field experiment.", "C) While investigating local residents' crime.", "D) While talking to police officers nearby."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】When did Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest find something really interesting in their\nresearch? 要求回到原文定位 really interesting 对应的具体阶段。\n【拆句】When did Anuj Shah and Michael LaForest find something really interesting in their\nresearch? 为 when 引导的特殊疑问句，主语为研究者，谓语为 find something really\ninteresting，结构清晰。\n【定位】But the really interesting finding came when the researchers stepped out of the lab, to conduct\na field experiment.\n但真正有趣的发现出现在研究者走出实验室、开展实地实验的时候。\n【选项】A) 转折后的内容权重高于转折前\nDuring lab-based studies. 对应的是前文提到的实验室研究，而原文用 But 转折指出真\n正有趣的发现来自 field experiment。\nB) ✓ 正确\nwhen the researchers stepped out of the lab, to conduct a field experiment ↔\nDuring a field experiment.\nDuring a field experiment. 与 when the researchers stepped out of the lab, to\nconduct a field experiment. 在时间上完全对应，是对原文的精确概括。\nC) 张冠李戴\nWhile investigating local residents' crime. 对应的是第4段中居民被问及犯罪的调查环\n节，而非第3段中 really interesting finding 发生的时机。\nD) 偷换概念\nWhile talking to police officers nearby. 中的 talking 是原文未描述的行为，警察只是\n发放传单和卡片，并未与居民交谈。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What happened in the areas around the housing developments subjected to the studies?", "options": ["A) They saw fewer crimes than the control developments.", "B) They witnessed a swifter elimination of illegal activities.", "C) Local police responded more quickly to criminal complaints than before.", "D) Local police related better to residents there than to those in other areas."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What happened in the areas around the housing developments subjected to the\nstudies? 要求提取实验后相关区域的具体变化。\n【拆句】What happened in the areas around the housing developments subjected to the\nstudies? 中 subjected to the studies 为过去分词短语作后置定语，修饰 housing\ndevelopments，整体询问某地发生的事件。\n【定位】Even more strikingly, the team found that immediately after the leaflet distribution, there\nwere fewer criminal complaints and arrests in the areas around the developments that had\nreceived the leaflets than around the control developments.\n更令人惊讶的是，研究小组发现，在发放传单后，收到传单的小区周边地区的犯罪投诉和逮\n捕数量都少于对照小区周边地区。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthere were fewer criminal complaints and arrests in the areas around the developments that had\nreceived the leaflets than around the control developments\n↔ They saw fewer crimes than the control developments.\nThey saw fewer crimes than the control developments. 中的 fewer crimes 对应\nfewer criminal complaints and arrests，control developments 与原文原词一致，概\n括准确。\nB) 过度推断\nThey witnessed a swifter elimination of illegal activities. 中的 swifter 是原文没有的\n速度含义，属于过度推断。\nC) 偷换概念\nLocal police responded more quickly to criminal complaints than before. 把\nfewer criminal complaints 错误地解释为警方响应速度提升，原文并未比较警方反应\n时间。\nD) 过度推断\nLocal police related better to residents there than to those in other areas. 把犯罪\n投诉和逮捕减少归结为警民关系更好，但原文并未评价警民关系。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2026-06-1"});
